    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm running an organic kale operation up in the PNW and I'm running into some nitrogen issues. My soil pH is sitting around 6.2 right now and I've been hitting it with compost, but the leaves are still looking pretty pale. I'm looking to supplement the nitrogen directly so I don't mess with the pH too much. Between bone meal and blood meal, which one would you recommend for a quick nitrogen boost without spiking the pH?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Considering your soil pH is around 6.2, bone meal is a better option than blood meal for a nitrogen boost. Bone meal is less acidic and is often available in a lower ammonium concentration, which is closer to the pH range your soil currently sits. Blood meal, on the other hand, is made from animal by-products and is quite acidic, which could potentially disrupt the pH balance. Additionally, bone meal contains calcium and phosphorus, which are also beneficial for your organic kale operation.",
