    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. quick follow up though, i've heard that bone meal breaks down way slower than blood meal and might not give me the immediate hit i need since the kale looks pretty bad right now. if i go with the blood meal to get the nitrogen up fast, how much should i be worried about the acidification over a single season? like is there a way to buffer it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, bone meal does break down slower, while blood meal is a more rapid release of nitrogen. If you choose to use blood meal, to mitigate the potential acidification, you can consider adding a lime or dolomitic limestone to your rotation or creating a buffer zone in your soil. This can help neutralize the acidic effects of the blood meal over time, but it will take some planning and monitoring to make this happen effectively.",
